## Setting up specimen records

The NAHPU default lists for `Specimen parts` and `Treatments` are intentionally
short. Before entering a large batch of specimens, configure the project lists
that your team will use in the field or collection room.

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the dashboard.
2. Tap/click the hamburger menu in the top left corner.
3. Select `Settings`.
4. Select `Specimens`.
5. Configure the `Use project ID` toggle. It is off by default, which uses a
   cataloger's initials and field number. At least one Cataloger must have
   personal field-number registration enabled and a current number.

    Turn on `Use project ID` to configure a prefix, the next catalog number,
    and a suffix. NAHPU concatenates those values directly. Turn on
    auto-increment to assign and advance the project number when a new specimen
    uses this mode.

    This choice is only the default. Open a specimen's field-ID editor to choose
    Personnel or Project for that specimen. A project can contain both types,
    but one specimen cannot have both identifiers.

    {% aside type="caution" %}

    Changing a prefix or suffix changes how existing project IDs display.
    Those identifiers may already have been used in exports or written on
    specimen labels. Double-check the values before confirming the change.

    {% /aside %}

6. Add `Tissue ID` (optional).

    Some museums assign separate tissue numbers for the same voucher specimen.
    You can set up a prefix and the next number here. NAHPU can then generate
    the next tissue number when you add a specimen part.

7. Add `Specimen types`.

    Add all preparation and tissue types you expect to use, including types you
    may only collect occasionally.

8. Add `Treatments`.

    Treatments are used on specimen parts. Examples include `None`, `ETOH`,
    `Formalin`, `LN2`, and `DMSO`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="tip" %}

Specimen types and treatments are user configs. They can be exported and
imported with project archives so another NAHPU installation can use the same
controlled lists and export behavior.

See [Settings](./settings) for the complete configuration map.

{% /aside %}

## Adding a new specimen record

{% steps %}
1. Navigate to the specimen page using the bottom navigation bar.

2. Click the `+` button to add a new specimen record.

    You can also use the three-dot menu and choose a duplicate action when you
    want to reuse part of the current specimen record.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    If you collect the same `Specimen part` for each new specimen, use
    `Duplicate part` to minimize repetitive entry. Review the copied part before
    saving because part-level IDs, timing, and treatments should still match the
    new specimen.

    {% /aside %}

3. Select `Cataloger`.

    This is the person responsible for cataloging the specimen. Depending on the
    project field ID mode, NAHPU can use the cataloger's field number or a
    project-level field number.

4. Select `Preparator`.

    NAHPU defaults the preparator to the cataloger. If a different person
    prepares the voucher or part, update the preparator field.

5. Select `Species`.

    Start typing to search the project's taxonomy list, then select the matching
    species. NAHPU populates the `Taxon Info` panel from that taxon record.

6. Select `ID confidence`.

    This tells later reviewers how confident you are in the identification and
    helps curators prioritize records that need review. The values are `Low`,
    `Medium`, and `High`.

7. Select the specimen `Condition`.

    Current condition values are `Freshly Euthanized`, `Good`, `Fair`, `Poor`,
    `Rotten`, `Released`, and `Unknown`.

8. Add the `Collection date`, `Collection time`, `Prep. date`, and `Prep. time`
   when available.

{% /steps %}

## General Records

The `General Records` card stores the core specimen metadata:

- `Museum ID`: optional museum catalog or accession identifier.
- Field ID: either the cataloger's field number or a formatted project number.
  Use the responsive field-ID editor to select the type for this specimen.
  Switching types removes the previous identifier; consumed numbers are not
  reused.
- `Cataloger`: the person who catalogs the specimen.
- `Preparator`: the person who prepares the specimen or voucher.
- `Species`: the selected project taxon.
- `ID Confidence`: confidence in the species identification.
- `ID Method`: optional method or evidence used for identification.
- `Condition`: condition of the specimen.
- `Collection date` and `Collection time`: when the specimen was collected.
- `Prep. date` and `Prep. time`: when the specimen was prepared.

## Taxonomy

The `Taxon Info` card is read from the species selected in `General Records`.
It shows class, order, and family in the specimen form. Select `View details` to
review the full taxonomy record, including:

- kingdom and phylum inferred from class;
- class, order, family, genus, species, authors, and common name;
- Red List category, CITES status, country status, and notes when present.

## Capture Records

Capture records connect a specimen to the collecting event, site, coordinate,
method, and collector that produced it.

- `Site ID`: the site associated with a collecting event.
- `Event ID`: the collecting event for the specimen. Changing the event clears
  event-dependent fields in this card except capture date and capture time.
- `Capture date`: the date the specimen was captured.
- `Capture time`: exact time, or a relative time when `Relative time` is enabled.
- `Relative time`: switches capture time to one of `Dawn`, `Morning`,
  `Afternoon`, `Dusk`, or `Night`.
- `Method`: the collecting effort/method from the selected event.
- `Method ID`: an optional trap, net, or other method-specific identifier.
- `Coordinate ID`: the coordinate record associated with the selected event.
- `Collector`: optional collector from the selected event personnel list.

## Measurements

Select the tab below to view measurement options for each taxonomic group.

{% tabs %}
{% tabitem label="Mammals" %}

- `Total length (mm)`: the total length from the tip of the nose to the tip of
  the tail.
- `Tail length (mm)`: the length of the tail from the base to the tip.
- `Head and body length (mm)`: auto-calculated from `Total length (mm) - Tail length (mm)`.
- `Tail/HB length`: auto-calculated from `Tail length (mm) / Head and body length (mm)`.
- `Hind foot length (mm)`: the length of the hind foot from the base to the tip of the claw.
- `Ear length (mm)`: ear length.
- `Weight (grams)`: specimen weight.
- `Accuracy`: measurement accuracy or reason measurements may be inaccurate.
- `Sex`: `Male`, `Female`, or `Unknown`.
- `Age`: `Adult`, `Subadult`, `Juvenile`, or `Unknown`.
- Male reproductive fields: testis position, testis length and width, and
  epididymis appearance.
- Female reproductive fields: vagina opening, pubic symphysis, reproductive
  stage, mammae condition, mammae counts, placental scar counts, embryo counts,
  and embryo crown-rump length.
- Bat fields, when enabled: forearm length, tibia length, echolocation type,
  maximum and minimum frequency, frequency at maximum energy, and duration.
- `Remarks`: additional notes about measurements.

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Birds" %}

- `Weight (grams)`: specimen weight.
- `Wingspan (mm)`: wingspan length.
- Soft-part colors: bill color, iris color, foot color, and tarsus color.
- `Sex`: `Male`, `Female`, or `Unknown`.
- `Brood patch`: whether a brood patch is present.
- `Bursa present`: whether a bursa is present. If present, record bursa length
  and width.
- `Skull ossification (%)`: selected from the available percentage list.
- `Fat`: fat category from `No Fat` through `Extremely Heavy`.
- `Stomach contents`: notes on stomach contents.
- Male gonad fields: testis length, width, and remarks.
- Female gonad fields: ovary length, ovary width, ovary appearance, ova sizes,
  oviduct width, oviduct appearance, and remarks.
- Molt fields: wing molt, tail molt, body molt, and molt remarks.
- Notes: habitat remarks and specimen remarks.

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Herpetofauna" %}

- `Sex`: `Male`, `Female`, or `Unknown`.
- `Age`: `Adult`, `Juvenile`, `Neonate`, `Metamorph`, or `Unknown`.
- `Weight (grams)`: specimen weight.
- `SVL (cm)`: snout-vent length.
- `Remarks`: additional information about the specimen.

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Fish and fossils" %}

    Measurement fields depend on the catalog format and the tabs available in
    your project. Record the fields shown for the selected format.

{% /tabitem %}


{% /tabs %}

## Specimen Parts

Use `Specimen Parts` for voucher preparations, tissues, swabs, parasites, and
other physical parts derived from the specimen. Each specimen can have multiple
parts.

Part fields include:

- `Tissue ID`: optional tissue identifier. NAHPU can repeat or generate the next
  configured tissue number.
- `QR/barcode ID`: optional unique ID for a tube, tag, or container. On mobile,
  NAHPU can scan a QR/barcode; it can also generate a UUID.
- `Preparation type`: user-defined specimen part type.
- `Counts`: number of parts or containers.
- `Treatment`: preservation treatment.
- `Additional treatment`: optional second treatment.
- `Storage`: storage medium or method.
- `Storage location`: freezer, cabinet, shelf, or container location.
- `Time taken`: time the part was taken, recommended for fresh tissues.
- `Preparator`: part-level preparator when different from the voucher preparator.
- `Date taken`: date the part was taken.
- `PMI`: post-mortem interval, for example `1:30`.
- `Museum permanent`: museum that permanently holds the part.
- `Museum loan`: museum receiving the part on loan.
- `Remarks`: notes specific to the part.

The part list displays the part type, count, barcode ID, preparator, tissue ID,
treatment, date/time, PMI, and whether a remark exists.

Parasite curation records likewise provide `Storage` and `Storage location` so
the preservation method and physical location can be recorded separately.

## Associated Data

Use `Associated Data` for specimen-linked digital data that is not better stored
as a specimen part or media record. Examples include GenBank accessions,
MorphoSource records, analysis files, or external database links.

Associated data fields include:

- `Data Type`: `Link` or `File`.
- `Name`: source or dataset name, such as `GenBank`.
- `Description`: accession number or description of the data.
- `Date`: date associated with the data.
- `URL`: external URL when the data type is `Link`.
- File selector: local file attachment when the data type is `File`.

## Media

Use `Media` for images or other media records associated with the specimen.
Media records are stored in the shared media system and linked to the specimen
through the specimen-media relationship.

## Managing specimen records

Open the three-dot menu on the specimen page to manage the current record:

- `Edit`: update the specimen, measurements, parts, associated data, or media.
- `Duplicate part`: create a new specimen using the current specimen's part
  information. Review IDs, dates, times, and treatments before saving.
- `Export specimen`: save a JSON record or an archive that includes specimen
  media.
- `Import specimen`: choose a new or existing target specimen, then map the
  imported event, site, and taxon references to records in the current project.
- `Delete record`: delete the specimen, its measurements, parts, associated
  data, and media links. The field number is not reused automatically.
- `Delete all records`: delete all specimens, measurements, parts, associated
  data, and specimen media in the current project.

Changing the event in `Capture Records` clears the event-dependent method,
collector, and coordinate values. Capture date and time are preserved. When a
record is imported, review these relationships and all generated identifiers
before continuing your cataloging workflow.
